<!-- OVERVIEW -->
<section class="content-section">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Hypertension is extremely common among older adults. More than two-thirds of people over age 65 have high blood pressure. As we age, our blood vessels naturally become stiffer, making it harder to control blood pressure.
    </p>
    <p>
      Managing hypertension is crucial for reducing the risk of heart attack, stroke, kidney disease, and cognitive decline in later years.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CHALLENGES -->
<section class="content-section">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Unique Challenges for Older Adults</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Age-related changes in blood vessels and heart function</li>
      <li>Multiple chronic conditions (diabetes, kidney disease, etc.)</li>
      <li>Polypharmacy (taking several medications)</li>
      <li>Increased sensitivity to medication side effects</li>
      <li>Risk of falls from low blood pressure or medication interactions</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Regular communication with healthcare providers is essential to balance blood pressure control with overall well-being.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- MANAGEMENT STRATEGIES -->
<section class="content-section">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Management Strategies</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Monitor blood pressure regularly at home and during doctor visits</li>
      <li>Take medications exactly as prescribed</li>
      <li>Eat a heart-healthy diet (low in salt, rich in fruits and vegetables)</li>
      <li>Stay physically active with safe, appropriate exercises</li>
      <li>Limit alcohol and avoid tobacco</li>
      <li>Manage other chronic conditions (diabetes, cholesterol, etc.)</li>
      <li>Report any side effects or symptoms to your healthcare provider</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WHY IT MATTERS -->
<section class="content-section">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Why It Matters</h2>
    <p>
      Good blood pressure control in older adults can help prevent heart attacks, strokes, kidney failure, and memory problems. It also supports independence and a better quality of life.
    </p>
    <p>
      Even small improvements in blood pressure can make a big difference in long-term health.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

